iterable_consume_errors.vlt:23:17: error: cannot spread a value of type `i64` into an array
what `for...of` iterates can be spread: arrays, strings, `Map`s, generators and iterables (values with a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method)
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:24:24: error: cannot spread `i64` elements into an array of `string`
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:25:24: error: `Array.from` takes an iterable or `{ length: n }`
pass an array, a string, a `Map`, a generator or a value with a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method, or write `Array.from({ length: n }, (_, i) => ...)`
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:26:17: error: function expressions are not supported
TypeScript allows this (`function (x) { ... }` as a value); Velt doesn't because arrow functions do the same without their own `this`; write an arrow function: `(x: T): R => { ... }` (a generator is written `function* (...)`)
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:29:30: error: a generator function expression cannot have type parameters
TypeScript allows this; Velt doesn't because a function value has one type; write a generic `function*` declaration and call it
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:32:27: error: a generator function expression cannot have a rest parameter
TypeScript allows this; Velt doesn't because calls through a function value pass each argument as written; take an array parameter (`xs: T[]`), or declare a `function*`
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:43:14: error: `walk` cannot be used inside the generator function expression it names
TypeScript allows this (the name of a function expression is in scope in its body); Velt doesn't because a function value cannot refer to itself; declare the generator with `function* name(...)` and refer to that
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:47:19: error: a generator must declare its return type
TypeScript allows this (it infers `Generator<T, void, unknown>`); Velt doesn't because the type of the values a generator yields is part of its signature, which is not inferred from its body; write `Generator<T>`, where `T` is the type of the values it yields
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:50:17: error: an object literal with a `[Symbol.iterator]()` method cannot have other members
TypeScript allows this; Velt doesn't because the iterable object holds only the method, which cannot reach the other members (`this` is not the object); use variables instead of the other members, or declare a class that `implements Iterable<T>` with fields
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:57:5: error: methods in object literals are not supported
TypeScript allows this; Velt doesn't because an object literal is plain data: its type has fields only (an iterable object literal, `{ *[Symbol.iterator]() { ... } }`, is the exception); write a property holding an arrow function (`name: (x: T): R => ...`), or declare a class
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:62:26: error: the `[Symbol.iterator]()` method of an object literal must return `Iterator<T>`
declare its result as `Iterator<T>` (an iterator class is returned as one), or make it a generator: `*[Symbol.iterator](): Generator<T>`
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:67:12: error: `async *` does not match the method's key
write `*[Symbol.iterator]()`, or `async *[Symbol.asyncIterator]()` for an async iterable
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:15:16: error: `this` cannot be used in the `[Symbol.iterator]()` method of an object literal
TypeScript allows this (`this` is the object); Velt doesn't because the object holds only the method, as a function that sees the variables around it; use those variables, or declare a class that `implements Iterable<T>` and reads its fields
iterable_consume_errors.vlt:37:20: error: a generator function expression cannot change `total` here
TypeScript allows this; Velt doesn't because `total` is also captured by an async closure that may run on another thread (or holds a promise), so it cannot be shared with the generators; share the value with `shared`: `const total = shared(...)` and `total.add(n)` / `total.set(v)`, or pass it in as a parameter
